// The npcbehave package: what a non-player character decides and how it
// moves. A program reaches it with 'import npcbehave', and gets four layers
// that know nothing of each other's insides and share only plain values:
//
//   deciding    BehaviorTree (with a Blackboard), UtilitySelector, StateMachine:
//               three ways to choose what to do this tick, each ticked at a
//               fixed rate and allocating nothing in a tick
//   sensing     SightCone, hearing (SoundEvent, Hearing) and Memory: what a
//               character can see, hear and still believe
//   steering    seek, flee, arrive, pursue, evade, Wander, PathFollower,
//               avoidObstacles, separation, alignment, cohesion, and the
//               blends WeightedBlend and PriorityBlend: each an acceleration
//   crowds      OrcaWorld: reciprocal collision avoidance for many agents
//   examples    patrol, graze, follow, flee and guard (archetypes.cone): short
//               characters made of the layers above
//
// CONVENTIONS. Metres and seconds. Steering and crowds work on the ground
// plane in 2-D: a Vec2 is (x, z) of the world, y up and ignored; a 3-D
// position becomes one with 'ground(v)' and comes back with 'lift(v, y)'.
// Sensing is 3-D (a sight line has a height). Every random choice is a hash of
// (seed, counter), so a run repeats exactly.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON. No navigation mesh, no physics, no scene: the
// world is asked through closures the caller passes at each call. A path
// comes from a 'PathQuery' ('fn(from: Vec2, to: Vec2, out: &mut List[Vec2]) bool'
// filling a corridor of points), a ray cast from 'fn(a: Vec3, b: Vec3) bool'
// (true when something blocks the line), the nearest walkable point from
// 'fn(p: Vec2) Vec2'. A navmesh package, a physics world or a test's straight
// line can each stand behind them. Closures are never stored: they are
// borrowed for the one call, so nothing here holds a reference into a world.
//
// METHODS (each is a published algorithm, named where it is written):
//   behaviour trees      the Isla/Champandard tick model: success, failure,
//                        running; composites with memory resume the running
//                        child; reactive variants restart each tick
//   utility AI           Dave Mark's infinite axis utility: considerations
//                        with response curves, multiplied with his
//                        compensation factor, plus inertia for the current action
//   state machine        Harel's statecharts reduced to a hierarchy of states
//                        with transitions found from the innermost state out
//   steering             Reynolds, "Steering Behaviors for Autonomous
//                        Characters" (GDC 1999); Millington, "AI for Games"
//                        (3rd ed., ch. 3: arrive, pursue, wander, path
//                        following, collision avoidance, blending, priority)
//   crowds               van den Berg, Guy, Lin and Manocha, "Reciprocal
//                        n-body collision avoidance" (ISRR 2009 / 2011), ORCA,
//                        with the incremental linear programs of RVO2
//
//   common.cone      statuses, the hash random numbers, Body2
//   blackboard.cone  Blackboard
//   btree.cone       BehaviorTree
//   utility.cone     Curve, Consideration, UtilitySelector
//   machine.cone     StateMachine
//   senses.cone      SightCone, SoundEvent, Hearing, Memory
//   steering.cone    the steering functions and the blends
//   orca.cone        OrcaWorld
//   archetypes.cone  the five example characters

mod npcbehave;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
